            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "# [[Find-the-Fuse-Box-or-Circuit-Breaker-Box|Look for the fuse box]] in your basement or garage. Check for a metal box with a door; inside you’ll see lots of glass fuses that screw into sockets. Typically, fuse boxes are located in basements, garages, laundry rooms, and attics.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "# Turn off the power and unplug the appliances on the blown circuit. Look for a large switch at the top of the panel and toggle it from “On” to “Off.” In addition to shutting off the main power, unplug the appliances that turned off when the fuse blew. That way, they won’t overload the replacement fuse when you turn on main power.<ref>https://worksafe.govt.nz/managing-health-and-safety/consumers/safe-living-with-electricity/electrical-wiring-in-your-home/</ref>",
                    "# Check the fuses for charred glass or broken filaments. See if there’s a diagram or labels on the inside of the panel door. If you’re lucky, you’ll be able to look up the room where the power went out and track down the corresponding fuse. Twist that fuse counterclockwise, pull it out from the socket, and look inside the glass for black marks or a broken filament wire.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "",
                    "#*The diagram will also list the fuse’s amperage, which you’ll need to know to purchase a replacement.",
                    "#*If the wire is intact and the fuse seems fine, double-check your diagram to ensure you have the correct fuse. If you've checked each fuse individually and none have blown, your car’s electrical system may need a mechanic’s attention.",
                    "#*If you’re not able to visually tell if the fuse has blown, then you may need to check the fuses with a [[Use-a-Multimeter|multimeter]] to determine if it’s working."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# [[Find-the-Fuse-Box-or-Circuit-Breaker-Box|Look for the fuse box]] in your basement or garage. Check for a metal box with a door; inside you’ll see lots of glass fuses that plug into sockets. Typically, fuse boxes are located in basements, garages, laundry rooms, and attics.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "# Turn off the power and unplug the appliances on the blown circuit. Look for a large switch at the top of the panel and toggle it from “On” to “Off.” In addition to shutting off main power, unplug the appliances that turned off when the fuse blew. That way, they won’t overload the replacement fuse when you turn on main power.<ref>https://worksafe.govt.nz/managing-health-and-safety/consumers/safe-living-with-electricity/electrical-wiring-in-your-home/</ref>",
                    "# Check the fuses for charred glass or broken filaments. See if there’s a diagram or labels on the inside of the panel door. If you’re lucky, you’ll be able to look up the room where power went out and track down the corresponding fuse. Twist that fuse counterclockwise, pull it out from the socket, and look inside the glass for black marks or a broken filament wire.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "#*The diagram will also list the fuse’s amperage, which you’ll need know in order to purchase a replacement.",
                    "#*If the wire is intact and the fuse seems fine, double check your diagram to ensure you have the correct fuse. If you've check each fuse individually and none have blown, your car’s electrical system may need a mechanic’s attention."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "26632659",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "19:16, 2 June 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,139 bytes)",
            "change": -7415,
            "summary": "(reorganized content, added more varied sources, simplified methods)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "A blown fuse can be annoying, but it actually helps prevent electrical damage and fire. Fortunately, fuses are inexpensive and easy to replace. If the power’s out in your home or car, you should be able to check the fuses simply by looking at them. Look inside the fuse in question for black charred marks or a broken filament wire. If you can’t see any obvious signs, you can always use a test light or multimeter to figure out if the fuse is working properly.",
                    "===Checking Your Home’s Fuse Box===",
                    "# [[Find-the-Fuse-Box-or-Circuit-Breaker-Box|Look for the fuse box]] in your basement or garage. Check for a metal box with a door; inside you’ll see lots of glass fuses that plug into sockets. Typically, fuse boxes are located in basements, garages, laundry rooms, and attics.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "#*If you have trouble finding your fuse box, check the utility meter outside your home. Try to follow the wire that leads from the meter. Fuse boxes and circuit breakers are usually located near where power enters the house.",
                    "#*Older homes have glass fuses that look like light bulbs. When fuses blow, they need to be replaced. Newer homes, on the other hand, have [[Change a Circuit Breaker|circuit breakers]] instead, which just need to be flipped and reset.",
                    "# Turn off the power and unplug the appliances on the blown circuit. Look for a large switch at the top of the panel and toggle it from “On” to “Off.” In addition to shutting off main power, unplug the appliances that turned off when the fuse blew. That way, they won’t overload the replacement fuse when you turn on main power.<ref>https://worksafe.govt.nz/managing-health-and-safety/consumers/safe-living-with-electricity/electrical-wiring-in-your-home/</ref>",
                    "#*If there’s no main power switch, you should see a large block at the top of the panel. Pull it out and check for “On” and “Off labels. If it’s labeled, reinsert it with the “Off” side facing up. If there’s no label, set the block aside while you replace the blown fuse.",
                    "# Check the fuses for charred glass or broken filaments. See if there’s a diagram or labels on the inside of the panel door. If you’re lucky, you’ll be able to look up the room where power went out and track down the corresponding fuse. Twist that fuse counterclockwise, pull it out from the socket, and look inside the glass for black marks or a broken filament wire.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "#*If your fuse box is unlabeled, check each fuse individually for charred marks or broken filaments. Once you’ve found the one that blew, label it! If you label your fuses every time you replace one, you’ll eventually have a full diagram of the box.",
                    "# Swap the blown fuse out for one with the same power rating. Look for a number on the fuse, which indicates its amp rating. Write down the number or take the blown fuse with you to the hardware store to ensure you get an identical match. Then, plug the replacement fuse in and turn it clockwise to lock it in place.<ref>https://worksafe.govt.nz/managing-health-and-safety/consumers/safe-living-with-electricity/electrical-wiring-in-your-home/</ref>",
                    "#*Fuse ratings vary by country, but common amperages include 15, 20, and 30.{{greenbox:'''Safety Warning:''' Never use a fuse with a higher amperage than the one that blew. Installing the wrong fuse can cause electrical damage or a fire.}}",
                    "# Turn the power back on to test the new fuse. Once you've plugged in the fuse, double-check to make sure you disconnected all of your electrical devices from the broken circuit. When you’re ready, flip the main fuse switch or reinsert the main block to restore power to your home. Then test the circuit by checking the lights or plugging in your electronic devices.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/wiring-outlets/troubleshooting-dead-outlets/</ref>",
                    "#*If your electronics still don’t work after replacing the fuse, shut down main power and make sure the fuse is tightly connected.",
                    "#*If the fuse blows again soon after you replaced it, you may be using more electrical devices than the circuit can handle. Try using fewer devices or unplug non-essential appliances when they’re not in use.<ref>https://www.bbc.com/bitesize/guides/z886b82/revision/2</ref>",
                    "#*If you still can’t find the problem, call an experienced electrician. It could mean that your home’s wiring is faulty.",
                    "===Troubleshooting Your Car’s Fuses===",
                    "# Look under the hood or dashboard for your vehicle’s fuse box. Many cars have 2 or more fuse boxes, but there's no universal standard for their placement. Most manufacturers place them near the car’s engine or battery, beneath the steering wheel, or inside the glove box. Look for a gray or black box; it may be labeled as “Fuses.”<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Check your car’s manual if you have trouble finding the fuse boxes. If you don’t have your manual, search online for “fuse box placement” plus your car’s year, make, and model.",
                    "# Find the fuse that controls the device that’s stopped working. Lift the latch on the side of the fuse box to loosen the cover. Check the inside of the lid for a diagram of the devices each fuse controls. Your manual may also include a diagram but, if all else fails, you can also find information on your specific vehicle online.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 18-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*The diagram will also list the fuse’s amperage, which you’ll need know in order to purchase a replacement.",
                    "#*Be sure to turn your car off before troubleshooting the fuse box.",
                    "# Remove the blown fuse from the box using fuse pullers or tweezers. Some cars and fuse replacement kits include small plastic fuse pullers, which you can use to pluck the fuse from the box. If you don’t have fuse pullers handy, a pair of tweezers will do the trick. In a pinch, you can also carefully pull the fuse from the box with your fingers.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 19-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Make sure your car is completely off before you pull out the blown fuse. Keep the keys out of the ignition to avoid accidentally putting it in “Run” or “Accessories” mode. Otherwise, you could get a nasty shock.",
                    "# Check the fuse for a broken filament or discoloration. Car fuses are small, so it’s a little tougher to find signs that one blew. Hold it up to the light and look closely inside for a thin wire that connects the 2 sides of the fuse. If the wire is broken, or if you see charred marks, the fuse has blown.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref>",
                    "#*If the wire is intact and the fuse seems fine, double check your diagram to ensure you have the correct fuse. If you've check each fuse individually and none have blown, your car’s electrical system may need a mechanic’s attention.",
                    "# Install a new fuse with an amperage that matches the one that blew. Head to an auto shop or major retailer's automotive department to find fuses for your car. Bring the blown fuse with you to ensure you get the right match. Once you’ve purchased the right replacement, simply insert it into the slot where you removed the blown fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 21.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 21-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 21.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 21.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 21.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Make sure the car is off before you set the fuse into the empty slot. Press down with a little pressure until it pops into place.",
                    "#*Car fuses are color-coded, so if the fuses are a different color, you know right away they aren’t a match. Additionally, be sure the prong shape of the new fuse matches the old one. If necessary, ask an employee at the store for help finding the right replacement.{{greenbox:'''Tip:''' If your car is disabled due to a blown fuse, you could swap out a matching fuse that controls a non-essential device. For instance, if the fuse that controls your ignition blew, you could temporarily use the one for your radio, as long as the 2 fuses are identical.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>}}",
                    "===Using Fuse Testing Devices===",
                    "# Check automotive fuses with a simple test light. Put your key in the ignition and turn it to accessories mode. Open the fuse box, then insert the tester’s probe into the tiny port on the face of a fuse. If the tester lights up, the fuse is working properly.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Using a test light is a great way to find a blown fuse when you can’t identify visual signs, such as a broken filament or black marks.",
                    "#*You can find car fuse test lights online, at auto shops, and in the automotive department at most major retailers. The tester has 1 or 2 prongs that fit into the tiny test ports on the outside face of all automotive fuses.{{greenbox:'''Tip:''' There’s no need to turn on the car’s engine, but be sure to test your car’s fuses in accessories mode. Otherwise, there won’t be an electrical current flowing to the fuses. Just remember to turn off the car before removing a fuse.}}",
                    "# Test a plug fuse with a multimeter if you don’t see signs that it blew. Set your multimeter to test resistance; the setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or “Ω.” Turn off the fuse box’s main power switch, then remove the fuse you’d like to test. Set the fuse glass-side-down on a non-conductive surface, such as a wood workbench or laminate counter, so the metal plug end faces up.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Plug fuses, which are often found in home fuse boxes, have threaded terminals that look like the ends of standard light bulbs. To test it, touch one of the multimeter’s probes to the very tip of the fuse’s terminal. Touch the other probe to the side of the terminal near where the threading stops.",
                    "#*If the multimeter displays a resistance between 0 and 5 Ω (ohms), the fuse is good. A higher resistance means a degraded or potentially blown fuse, and a reading of OL (over limit) means the fuse is definitely blown.<ref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#*Like an automotive test light, using a multimeter to test a home plug fuse is a good solution if you suspect a blown fuse but can’t find any clear visual signs.",
                    "# Use a multimeter to test cylindrical fuses in electronic devices. If your electronic instrument or appliance is on the fritz, remove the fuse according to the manufacturer’s instructions. Typically, electronic devices use tube-shaped fuses with a glass body and terminals at each end. Set your multimeter to test resistance, and place the fuse on a non-conductive surface.<ref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3483613/</ref>",
                    "#*Touch one of the multimeter’s probes to each of the fuse’s terminals. As with plug fuses, a resistance reading of 0 to 5 Ω means the fuse works. A higher reading or a reading of OL means the fuse is bad.",
                    "#*You can inspect cylindrical fuses for broken filaments or charred marks just like automotive and plug fuses. However, if you can’t see clearly inside the fuse, testing it with a multimeter can help you figure out whether or not it works.",
                    "*Keep a few extra fuses on hand in your car or home so you can make a quick repair when necessary.",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, always turn off the electricity before touching any fuses."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "When a potentially dangerous electrical surge occurs, a fuse can “blow.” Breaking the electrical current protects the valuable components in your car and home from overheating. Even better, fuses are cheap and can be replaced without any special mechanical knowledge. You can test most fuses simply by looking at them, or you can perform more thorough tests with devices such as a test light or multimeter. Once you have identified a broken fuse, replace it with an identical fuse to get your power running again.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "===Locating and Inspecting the Fuses===",
                    "#Look under the hood or dashboard for car fuses. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Most manufacturers place them near the car’s engine or battery. Some hide fuses boxes beneath the steering wheel or even inside the passenger side glove box. Look for a gray or black box, usually labeled as “Fuses.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' The fuse boxes can be in different locations. You may have 1 underneath the car’s hood and another inside the car.}}",
                    "#*Check your car’s manual if you aren’t sure where the fuse boxes are. Sometimes they are well-hidden in tricky locations you wouldn’t expect, such as in the trunk or under the rear seat.",
                    "#*If you don’t have your manual, search for your car’s make and model online.",
                    "#Find the electrical controls if you’re looking in your home. If you think car fuses are easy to find and remove, home fuses might be easier. To find the box, locate the utility meter outside your home. Follow the wire from the meter as it enters your home. This will lead you to a big, gray box with a bunch of switches and glass tubes in it.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*Expect the fuse box to be on an outside wall in an out of the way spot. The most common sports for it are in the garage and in the basement. If you’re unable to find it, ask your utility company for help.",
                    "#*Older homes have glass fuses that look like lightbulbs. Newer homes have [[Change a Circuit Breaker|circuit breakers]] instead, which are even easier to fix.",
                    "#Remove the fuse box lid and read the label on its back. Locate the small latch jutting out from the side of the box and pull it toward you to pop off the lid. Most fuse boxes have a diagram on the inside part of the lid, so use that to figure out which fuses you are looking at. The chart will show you what system each fuse powers, its amp rating, and its corresponding location inside the box.{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>",
                    "#*For example, a square in the diagram’s upper left corner might have the word “radio” printed on it, so you know the fuse for the radio is in the upper left corner of the box.",
                    "#*If your lid doesn’t have a diagram, look for one in your car manual. For home fuse boxes, consider labeling the fuses yourself as you figure out what rooms they control.",
                    "#Leave the fuse in the box as you look for broken wires. Don’t remove the fuse yet, since it could be dangerous if the electricity is on! Instead, take a quick glance at the fuses with a flashlight. If the inside of a fuse is black, you know for sure that it is broken. If the fuse case is still clear, check if it the small metal filament inside of it is whole.{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>{{greenbox: '''Note:''' Removing a working fuse can erase data stored in the car’s diagnostic system. This is not an issue unless you need to get your car repaired, as the mechanic will use that data to assess the issue.}}",
                    "#*If you’re having a hard time identifying the damaged fuses, you can turn off the electricity to the fuse box and then pull out the fuses one by one.",
                    "#*The most common type of fuse is a 2-pronged plastic chip often used in cars. It will have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs.",
                    "#*An older type of fuse is the transparent glass or plastic cylinder. It has a wire running end to end and is used in older homes and some cars.",
                    "",
                    "===Using a Test Light===",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle or an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 7 Version 5.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' If you have a multimeter, you can use it instead of a test light.}}",
                    "#*Never test car wiring with an incandescent bulb that draws power from the circuit. This style of test light can trigger the airbags, causing massive car damage.",
                    "#Clamp the black grounding lead to a piece of metal. The clamp will be at the end of the test light opposite from the incandescent bulb. Squeeze the end of the clamp to open the jaws. Then, slide the jaws over a metal component near the fuse box. Any metal piece will work, so look for a nearby pipe in your home or exposed piece of metal under your car’s hood, for instance.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*A great place to clamp the grounding lead in a car is on the battery’s negative terminal. It is the black terminal and may be marked with a “-” sign.",
                    "#*Note that newer LED models may not have a clamp and you do not need to attach them to anything.",
                    "#Turn on the electricity to activate the electrical system. If you’re testing a car fuse, turn the key in the ignition to turn on the car. For home fuses, flip the main switch that controls power to your entire home. The switch is usually bigger than anything else in the fuse box and is easily noticeable near its top or sides.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Make sure the engine stays off if you’re in the car. You don’t need a full electrical current passing through the fuses while you’re at work, so play it safe.",
                    "#*Test the electricity to make sure it is on. Try turning on your car’s radio, for instance, or activating light bulbs near your home fuse box.",
                    "#Test the light by touching its probe to a power source. You can operate the test light by pressing the tip down onto different electrical components. To test the light, you will need to find something you know is in working order and has power running through it. No matter what kind of fuse you are testing, you probably have something nearby. The test light will glow as soon as you touch it to an active device.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*In a car, the easiest place to reach is often the battery’s positive terminal. It will be red with a “+” sign marked on it.",
                    "#*You can also touch the probe to a working fuse, a wall outlet, or a light socket.",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On a 2-pronged fuse, these holes are on the upper part of the fuse. Slide the end of the probe into a port to test it, then repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' If your car component doesn’t work but the fuse still does, the problem may be somewhere else. You can use the probe to test objects like brake switches.}}",
                    "#*For cylindrical fuses, touch the probe against both ends.",
                    "#*If neither end causes the bulb to light up, make sure power is running to the fuse box. Then, check that the black lead wire in the fuse box is grounded.",
                    "",
                    "===Testing with a Multimeter===",
                    "#Turn off the electricity to the fuse box. If you’re dealing with car fuses, turning off the engine shuts off the electrical supply. For homes, flip the main switch controlling electricity to your home. Testing on a live circuit is dangerous, so make sure the power is off. Try activating the car’s radio or devices in your home to see if they turn on.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 12-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*If you have an electric car, make sure it isn’t connected to a charging port.",
                    "#Remove the fuse by pulling it out of the box. Home fuses are just big light bulbs, so turn one counterclockwise to remove it. Car fuses are much smaller, but needle-nose pliers are the perfect size for dealing with those tiny things. Clamp the pliers onto the fuse and pull upward to lift it out of the box.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 13-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*You may be able to lift car fuses out with your fingernails, but prying out these miniscule fuses by hand can be a challenge.",
                    "#*A regular pair of pliers or tweezers may work, or you can even use the end of a paperclip to leverage a car fuse.",
                    "#*If you plan on testing more than 1 fuse, consider taking a photograph first so you know exactly where each fuse goes.",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current into fuse, testing how much electricity passes through it. Point the multimeter’s dial to this setting before beginning the test.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the option listed as Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>}}",
                    "#Touch the 2 probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end, a negative and positive one. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout will change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Your multimeter will have a dial if it is an analog model or a button if it is digital. You can use this to calibrate the meter back to 0 if it gives you a false reading.",
                    "#Touch the probes to the ends of the fuse. For a car fuse, place a multimeter probe on each of the metal terminals. For glass fuses, place a lead on each end of the glass tube. Juggling these wires and the fuse at the same time is a little tricky at first, so find a non-conductive surface to set the fuse on if you have one available. If the fuse is till good, the multimeter’s needle or number will change like it did when both probes were touching.<ref>https://www.freeasestudyguides.com/check-blown-fuse.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 16-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Some good places to put the fuse on are wood, laminate, and plastic surfaces.",
                    "#*You can pop a good fuse right back into the fuse box., but a broken fuse needs to be replaced with an identical one.",
                    "",
                    "===Replacing a Car Fuse===",
                    "#Shut the car’s power off by turning the ignition key. The car’s engine needs to be off, not running while you install the replacement. A running current can lead to a painful shock. Take precautions to avoid turning a simple repair into a costly hospital visit.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 17-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 17 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Take the key out of the ignition to make sure the car is off. You can also try turning on the radio to see if the power is off.",
                    "#*If you have an electric car, detach any charging cables from outlets.",
                    "#Pull the cover off of the fuse box to check the diagram. Lift the latch on the side of the fuse box to loosen the cover. You will see a diagram somewhere on the box, usually on the lid. This diagram shows you where each fuse is located in the box as well as their amperage rating.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 18-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*You will need to know the amperage rating in order to get a new fuse.",
                    "#*If you can’t locate a diagram, look in the owner’s manual or repair manual for more information.",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the box using needle-nose pliers. Old fuses are easy to remove. The biggest challenge is their small size, but needle-nose pliers are designed to grasp small components. Fit the pliers on the fuse, then lift the fuse out of the box.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 19-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*All the fuses are different sizes, so use the size along with the diagram to pick the correct fuse. Inspect it for damage before replacing it.",
                    "#Purchase an identical replacement fuse. Head to an auto supply store to find fuses for your car. Getting there may be tricky if your car is disabled due to a blown fuse. However, you should bring the fuse with you to ensure you get a proper match. The new fuse should be the same type, shape, and amp rating as your old fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 20.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' Car fuses are color-coded, so if the fuses are a different color, you know right away they aren’t a match.}}",
                    "#*The amp rating is the number written on the fuse. If the fuse has the wrong amp rating, it can short-circuit or cause damage to your car.",
                    "#*Transparent cylindrical fuses come in 2 types, a straight fast-blow wire and a coiled slow-blow wire. Remember to match the wire shapes in your new and old fuse.",
                    "#Pop the new fuse into your fuse box. Setting the new fuse in the box is painless. Make sure the car is still off, then set the fuse in the empty slot. Press down with a little pressure until it pops into place. You’re already done and can turn on your car to test it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 21.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 21-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 21.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 21.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 21.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Glass fuses sometimes need to be installed by pushing 1 end into place at a time.",
                    "",
                    "===Replacing a Home Fuse===",
                    "#Swap the fuse out for one with the same power rating. Check the fuse to find a number on it indicating its amp rating. Getting a matching fuse is very important for safety reasons. If you can, take the broken fuse with you to the store to ensure you get an identical match. Then, plug the fuse in and turn it clockwise to lock it in place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ewD2FHev_pk&feature=youtu.be&t=159</ref>",
                    "#*Check online for replacement fuses or visit your nearest hardware store.",
                    "#*The wrong fuse increases the chances of electrical damage or even house fires. Never use a stronger fuse than the one you have. It will overload the circuit with extra power.",
                    "#*Plug fuses are commonly rated as 15, 20, and 30-amp. The 15-amp one is for lighting, the 20 is for outlets, and the 30 is for heavy appliances.",
                    "#Turn the power back on to test the new fuse. Once you have the fuse plugged in, double-check to make sure you disconnected all your electrical devices from the broken circuit. That means your toaster, your phone charger, and anything else you can think of. When you’re ready, flip the main fuse switch to restore power to your home. Test the circuit by going back and plugging all your electronics back in.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*If the electricity doesn’t work at all, unplug everything again and take another look at your fuse box. Check that the fuses fit tightly into their slots before flipping the main control switch back on.",
                    "#Plug your devices in individually if the fuse blows again. Sometimes the power will go out not long after you replace a blown fuse. That usually means that one of your devices is overloading the circuit. To find out which one it is, purchase another replacement fuse. After installing the new fuse, plug each item in one at a time until the fuse blows again.<ref>https://www.bbc.com/bitesize/guides/z886b82/revision/2</ref>",
                    "#*Unplug each device before plugging another one back in. Your devices could be faulty instead of the fuse itself. If none of them trip the fuse, then you might have overloaded the circuit with too many devices.",
                    "#*If you still can’t find the problem, call an experienced electrician. It could mean that your electrical wires are faulty.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "*Fuses age like any other electrical component. They need to be replaced over time, but it doesn’t mean that your car or home power supply is broken.",
                    "*If you need to drive your car when it has a blown fuse, replace the missing fuse with one from a nonessential system like your radio. Make sure both fuses have identical amp ratings.",
                    "*Keep a few extra fuses stored in your car or home so you can make a quick repair when necessary.",
                    "",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, turn off the electricity before touching any fuses.",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "===Locating and Inspecting the Fuses===",
                    "*Flashlight",
                    "",
                    "===Using a Test Light===",
                    "*Test light",
                    "",
                    "===Testing with a Multimeter===",
                    "*Multimeter",
                    "*Needle-nose pliers",
                    "",
                    "===Replacing a Car Fuse===",
                    "*New fuse",
                    "*Needle-nose pliers",
                    "",
                    "===Replacing a Home Fuse===",
                    "*Identical replacement fuse",
                    "*Flashlight",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "26555796",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "21:58, 15 May 2019",
