            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "When a potentially dangerous electrical surge occurs, a fuse can “blow.” Breaking the electrical current protects the valuable components in your car and home from overheating. Even better, fuses are cheap and can be replaced without any special mechanical knowledge. You can test most fuses simply by looking at them, or you can perform more thorough tests with devices such as a test light or multimeter. Once you have identified a broken fuse, replace it with an identical fuse to get your power running again.",
                    "",
                    "===Locating and Inspecting the Fuses===",
                    "#Look under the hood or dashboard for car fuses. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Most manufacturers place them near the car’s engine or battery. Some hide fuses boxes beneath the steering wheel or even inside the passenger side glove box. Look for a gray or black box, usually labeled as “Fuses.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' The fuse boxes can be in different locations. You may have 1 underneath the car’s hood and another inside the car.}}",
                    "#*Check your car’s manual if you aren’t sure where the fuse boxes are. Sometimes they are well-hidden in tricky locations you wouldn’t expect, such as in the trunk or under the rear seat.",
                    "#Find the electrical controls if you’re looking in your home. If you think car fuses are easy to find and remove, home fuses might be easier. To find the box, locate the utility meter outside your home. Follow the wire from the meter as it enters your home. This will lead you to a big, gray box with a bunch of switches and glass tubes in it.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*Expect the fuse box to be on an outside wall in an out of the way spot. The most common sports for it are in the garage and in the basement. If you’re unable to find it, ask your utility company for help.",
                    "#*Older homes have glass fuses that look like lightbulbs. Newer homes have [[Change a Circuit Breaker|circuit breakers]] instead, which are even easier to fix.",
                    "#Remove the fuse box lid and read the label on its back. Locate the small latch jutting out from the side of the box and pull it toward you to pop off the lid. Most fuse boxes have a diagram on the inside part of the lid, so use that to figure out which fuses you are looking at. The chart will show you what system each fuse powers, its amp rating, and its corresponding location inside the box.{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>",
                    "#*If your lid doesn’t have a diagram, look for one in your car manual. For home fuse boxes, consider labeling the fuses yourself as you figure out what rooms they control.",
                    "#Leave the fuse in the box as you look for broken wires. Don’t remove the fuse yet, since it could be dangerous if the electricity is on! Instead, take a quick glance at the fuses with a flashlight. If the inside of a fuse is black, you know for sure that it is broken. If the fuse case is still clear, check if it the small metal filament inside of it is whole.{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>{{greenbox: '''Note:''' Removing a working fuse can erase data stored in the car’s diagnostic system. This is not an issue unless you need to get your car repaired, as the mechanic will use that data to assess the issue.}}",
                    "#*If you’re having a hard time identifying the damaged fuses, you can turn off the electricity to the fuse box and then pull out the fuses one by one.",
                    "#*The most common type of fuse is a 2-pronged plastic chip often used in cars. It will have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs.",
                    "#*An older type of fuse is the transparent glass or plastic cylinder. It has a wire running end to end and is used in older homes and some cars.",
                    "===Using a Test Light===",
                    "#Clamp the black grounding lead to a piece of metal. The clamp will be at the end of the test light opposite from the incandescent bulb. Squeeze the end of the clamp to open the jaws. Then, slide the jaws over a metal component near the fuse box. Any metal piece will work, so look for a nearby pipe in your home or exposed piece of metal under your car’s hood, for instance.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*A great place to clamp the grounding lead in a car is on the battery’s negative terminal. It is the black terminal and may be marked with a “-” sign.",
                    "#Turn on the electricity to activate the electrical system. If you’re testing a car fuse, turn the key in the ignition to turn on the car. For home fuses, flip the main switch that controls power to your entire home. The switch is usually bigger than anything else in the fuse box and is easily noticeable near its top or sides.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Make sure the engine stays off if you’re in the car. You don’t need a full electrical current passing through the fuses while you’re at work, so play it safe.",
                    "#*Test the electricity to make sure it is on. Try turning on your car’s radio, for instance, or activating light bulbs near your home fuse box.",
                    "#Test the light by touching its probe to a power source. You can operate the test light by pressing the tip down onto different electrical components. To test the light, you will need to find something you know is in working order and has power running through it. No matter what kind of fuse you are testing, you probably have something nearby. The test light will glow as soon as you touch it to an active device.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*In a car, the easiest place to reach is often the battery’s positive terminal. It will be red with a “+” sign marked on it.",
                    "#*You can also touch the probe to a working fuse, a wall outlet, or a light socket.",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On a 2-pronged fuse, these holes are on the upper part of the fuse. Slide the end of the probe into a port to test it, then repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' If your car component doesn’t work but the fuse still does, the problem may be somewhere else. You can use the probe to test objects like brake switches.}}",
                    "#*If neither end causes the bulb to light up, make sure power is running to the fuse box. Then, check that the black lead wire in the fuse box is grounded.",
                    "===Testing with a Multimeter===",
                    "#Turn off the electricity to the fuse box. If you’re dealing with car fuses, turning off the engine shuts off the electrical supply. For homes, flip the main switch controlling electricity to your home. Testing on a live circuit is dangerous, so make sure the power is off. Try activating the car’s radio or devices in your home to see if they turn on.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 12-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*If you have an electric car, make sure it isn’t connected to a charging port.",
                    "#Remove the fuse by pulling it out of the box. Home fuses are just big light bulbs, so turn one counterclockwise to remove it. Car fuses are much smaller, but needle-nose pliers are the perfect size for dealing with those tiny things. Clamp the pliers onto the fuse and pull upward to lift it out of the box.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 13-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*You may be able to lift car fuses out with your fingernails, but prying out these miniscule fuses by hand can be a challenge.",
                    "#*A regular pair of pliers or tweezers may work, or you can even use the end of a paperclip to leverage a car fuse.",
                    "#*If you plan on testing more than 1 fuse, consider taking a photograph first so you know exactly where each fuse goes.",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current into fuse, testing how much electricity passes through it. Point the multimeter’s dial to this setting before beginning the test.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the option listed as Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>}}",
                    "#Touch the 2 probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end, a negative and positive one. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout will change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the probes to the ends of the fuse. For a car fuse, place a multimeter probe on each of the metal terminals. For glass fuses, place a lead on each end of the glass tube. Juggling these wires and the fuse at the same time is a little tricky at first, so find a non-conductive surface to set the fuse on if you have one available. If the fuse is till good, the multimeter’s needle or number will change like it did when both probes were touching.<ref>https://www.freeasestudyguides.com/check-blown-fuse.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 16-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Some good places to put the fuse on are wood, laminate, and plastic surfaces.",
                    "#*You can pop a good fuse right back into the fuse box., but a broken fuse needs to be replaced with an identical one.",
                    "===Replacing a Home Fuse===",
                    "#*If the electricity doesn’t work at all, unplug everything again and take another look at your fuse box. Check that the fuses fit tightly into their slots before flipping the main control switch back on.",
                    "*Fuses age like any other electrical component. They need to be replaced over time, but it doesn’t mean that your car or home power supply is broken.",
                    "*If the replacement fuse blows shortly after installation, your car or home may have a more serious problem. Call an experienced mechanic or electrician for advice.",
                    "*If you need to drive your car when it has a blown fuse, replace the missing fuse with one from a nonessential system like your radio. Make sure both fuses have identical amp ratings.",
                    "*Keep a few extra fuses stored in your car or home so you can make a quick repair when necessary.",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, turn off the electricity before touching any fuses.",
                    "===Locating and Inspecting the Fuses===",
                    "===Replacing a Car Fuse===",
                    "===Replacing a Home Fuse==="
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "When a potentially dangerous electrical surge occurs, a car fuse can “blow.” Breaking the electrical current protects the valuable components in your car and home from overheating. Even better, fuses are cheap and can be replaced without any special mechanical knowledge. You can test most fuses simply by looking at them, or you can perform more thorough tests with electronics such as a test light or multimeter. Once you have identified a broken fuse, you can replace it with an identical fuse to get your power running again.",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs on Car Fuses===",
                    "#Look under the car’s hood or dash board. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Many manufacturers choose to keep them near the car’s engine or battery. Other manufacturer’s like to hide the fuse boxes inside the car, beneath the steering wheel. Look for a gray or black box, usually with the word “Fuses” printed on it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' The fuse boxes can be in different locations. You may have 1 underneath the car’s hood and another inside the car.}}",
                    "#*Pop your vehicle’s hood to find the box located near the battery or engine.",
                    "#Check your car or repair manual if you can’t find the fuse boxes. These manuals come with new cars and are brimming with time-saving information, such as the location of the fuse boxes. This is helpful if your fuse boxes seem to be hidden pretty well. Some car manufacturers put fuse boxes in tricky locations such as in the glove box, near the brake pedal, in the trunk, or under the rear seat.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 2 Version 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If a part of your car isn’t working properly, use the manual to go directly to the fuse responsible for the part that lost power.",
                    "#Remove the lid from the fuse box by pulling the latch. Look for a small latch jutting out from the lid’s side. Slide your fingers underneath the latch. Then, pull the latch towards you to easily pop the lid off of the fuse box.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Some lids need to be rotated before they can be pulled off of the fuse box.",
                    "#Read the fuse box label on the back of the lid. Most fuse boxes have a diagram on the inside portion of the lid. It will list what system each fuse powers, it’s amp rating, and its corresponding location in the fuse box. This is very useful, since each fuse box will have around 20 fuses.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 4 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If your lid doesn’t have a diagram, you can always find the fuse box diagram in the manual.",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected in the fuse box. Don’t remove the fuse yet! This can be dangerous if the car is on. Removing a functioning fuse may also cause minor problems to the connected systems. Instead, examine each fuse while it is in place inside the box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5 Version 5.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Note:''' Removing a working fuse can erase data stored in the car’s diagnostic system. This is not an issue unless you need to get your car repaired, as the mechanic will use that data to assess the issue.}}",
                    "#*If you find yourself having a hard time identifying damaged fuses, you can pull fuses out of the box. Make sure the car’s ignition is turned off first.",
                    "#Check fuses for broken wires or scorch marks. Take a flashlight with you so the damaged components are easier to see. There are 2 types of fuses you may encounter, but they both operate the same way. If the inside of the fuse is black, you know it is broken. If it is clear, look inside it to see if the wire is still whole.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*The most common type of fuse is a 2-pronged plastic chip. It will have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs.",
                    "#*An older type of fuse is the transparent glass or plastic cylinder. It has a wire running end to end.",
                    "===Using a Test Light on a Car Fuse===",
                    "#Clamp the black grounding lead to a piece of metal. The clamp will be at the end of the test light opposite from the incandescent bulb. Squeeze the end of the clamp to open the jaws. Then, slide the jaws over a metal component near the fuse box. Any metal piece on the car will work, so you don’t need to stretch for any components out of your range.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*A great place to clamp the grounding lead is on the battery’s negative terminal. It is the black terminal and may be marked with a “-” sign.",
                    "#Turn the key in the ignition to start the car’s electrical system. Turn the key so it is in the “Run” position. This should activate the car’s electrical systems, such as the radio and door lights, without starting the engine. You can test this by attempting to turn on the radio if it isn’t already on.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Make sure the engine stays off. You don’t want a full electrical current coursing through the car’s parts while you’re working on them.",
                    "#Test the light by touching its probe to a power source. You can operate the test light by pressing the tip down onto different electrical components. To test the light, you will need to find something you know is in working order and has power running through it. The easiest option, if you can reach it, is the car battery’s positive terminal. The test light should glow immediately.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*The positive terminal will be red with a “+” sign marked on it.",
                    "#*You can also touch the probe to a working fuse or the positive battery cable’s metal connectors.",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On 2-prong fuses, you can find these holes on the top side at both ends.. Slide the end of the probe into the fuse’s port. Repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' If your car component doesn’t work but the fuse still does, the problem may be somewhere else. You can use the probe to test objects like brake switches.}}",
                    "#*If neither end causes the bulb to light up, make sure the black lead wire is grounded and that power is running to the fuse box.",
                    "===Testing a Car Fuse with a Multimeter===",
                    "#Turn the ignition key to shut the car off. Turning off the engine shuts off the car’s electrical supply. If you have an electric car, also make sure the car is not connected to a charging port. Keep the power off to avoid a nasty electric shock!<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 12-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Always make sure the car is off before using the multimeter, since live circuits are dangerous.",
                    "#Remove the fuse with a pair of needle-nose pliers. Needle-nose pliers are small and have long noses, making them perfect for dealing with those tiny fuses in your car. First, open the fuse box if you haven’t already by pulling the latch. Squeeze the handles to open up the pliers and clamp them onto the fuse. Pull the fuse upwards to lift it out of the fuse box without any struggling.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 13-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*You may be able to lift the fuses out with your fingernails, but prying out these miniscule fuses by hand can be a challenge.",
                    "#*A regular pair of pliers or tweezers may work, or you can even use the end of a paperclip to leverage the fuse.",
                    "#*If you plan on testing more than 1 fuse in a car's fuse box, take a photograph first so you know exactly where each fuse goes.",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, testing how much current goes through. Make sure the mutimeter’s dial points to this setting.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the option listed as Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>}}",
                    "#Touch the 2 probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout should change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the probes to the ends of the fuse. Hold onto the fuse as you press the probes against the metal terminals on the fuse’s bottom end. Trying to juggle the wires and the small fuse at the same time can be tricky at first, but keep at it. If the fuse is till good, the multimeter’s needle or number will change like it did when both probes were touching.<ref>https://www.freeasestudyguides.com/check-blown-fuse.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 16-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*You can pop a good fuse right back into the fuse box.",
                    "#*If the number doesn’t change, your fuse is busted. You will need to replace it.",
                    "===Testing and Replacing a Home Fuse===",
                    "#Find the fuse box close to your electric meter. Fuses boxes are always close to the utility meter outside your home. If you can find the utility meter, follow the electrical wires as they pass through your home. Look for a big, gray box with a bunch of switches or glass tubes in it. Expect it to be on an outside wall in your home in an out of the way area.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*The most common spots for fuse boxes are in the garage and in the basement. If you’re unable to find it, contact your local utility company for more information.",
                    "#Turn off the power using the main switch in the fuse box. Open the fuse box cover, then look for the switch controlling the main circuit in your home. It shuts down every electrical device in your home, so be prepared for the darkness by carrying a flashlight. Make sure the power is completely off so you don’t have to worry about the risk of electrical shock. Also, unplug anything connected to the circuit controlled by the fuse you wish to replace.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*Test the power by attempting to turn on the electricity in different rooms. The lights and any plugged-in devices won’t turn on if the power is out.",
                    "#*The switch will usually be labeled in your fuse box. If the fuses aren’t labeled in your home, look for the biggest switch or keep flipping switches until you find the one you need. Consider labeling the fuse box components so you know where to look next time.",
                    "#Twist the fuse counterclockwise to remove it from the box. Home fuses screw in like light bulbs, which makes them super easy to remove and replace. The fuses are usually labeled by room, so find the one that controls the part of your home that lost power. Take it out of the box to replace it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ewD2FHev_pk&feature=youtu.be&t=97</ref>",
                    "#*More modern homes use [[Change a Circuit Breaker|circuit breakers]] instead of old-fashioned fuses. To reactivate a tripped breaker, all you have to do is flip it back to the “on” position. If it still doesn’t work, you can unscrew it, pull the wires out, and replace it.",
                    "#Test different fuses by if you don’t know which one broke. So your fuse box isn’t labeled and you’re stuck staring at a bunch of fuses that all look the same. Select and remove a fuse as you normally would, but then turn the power back on. Also, turn on the main switch and check your home to see which circuit didn’t reactivate. By doing this with each fuse, you can find the one responsible for the power outage.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHQMCovwwAo&feature=youtu.be&t=147</ref>",
                    "#*For example, if you’re looking for the kitchen fuse, remove a fuse and test it. If it deactivates a different room, put it back in and try again. Remember to turn the main power off each time you touch the fuses.",
                    "#*A blown fuse may also look scorched or broken. If you notice a damaged fuse, you can zero in on the source of the problem.",
                    "#*If the electricity doesn’t work at all, you most likely didn’t activate the electricity. Unplug everything again, then take another look at your fuse box. Make sure the fuse all fit tightly into their slots before flipping the main control switch to on.",
                    "*Fuses age like any other car part. Over time, they will fail. It doesn’t mean your car has a major problem.",
                    "*If the replacement fuse blows shortly after installation, your car’s electrical system may have a more serious issue. Consult an mechanic.",
                    "*If you need to drive the car, such as to pick up a new fuse, replace the broken fuse with a fuse from a nonessential system, such as the radio. Make sure both fuses have identical ampere ratings.",
                    "*Keep a few fuses stored in your car so you can make a quick repair when necessary.",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, make sure the car’s power is turned off before handling a multimeter or any fuses.",
                    "",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs of Damage===",
                    "===Replacing a Fuse===",
                    "===Testing and Replacing a Home Fuse==="
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "26546910",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "00:58, 14 May 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(23,377 bytes)",
            "change": 5516,
            "summary": "(Adding section on home fuses.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "When a potentially dangerous electrical surge occurs, a car fuse can “blow.” Breaking the electrical current protects the valuable components in your car and home from overheating. Even better, fuses are cheap and can be replaced without any special mechanical knowledge. You can test most fuses simply by looking at them, or you can perform more thorough tests with electronics such as a test light or multimeter. Once you have identified a broken fuse, you can replace it with an identical fuse to get your power running again.",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs on Car Fuses===",
                    "===Using a Test Light on a Car Fuse===",
                    "===Testing a Car Fuse with a Multimeter===",
                    "===Replacing a Car Fuse===",
                    "",
                    "===Testing and Replacing a Home Fuse===",
                    "#Find the fuse box close to your electric meter. Fuses boxes are always close to the utility meter outside your home. If you can find the utility meter, follow the electrical wires as they pass through your home. Look for a big, gray box with a bunch of switches or glass tubes in it. Expect it to be on an outside wall in your home in an out of the way area.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*The most common spots for fuse boxes are in the garage and in the basement. If you’re unable to find it, contact your local utility company for more information.",
                    "#Turn off the power using the main switch in the fuse box. Open the fuse box cover, then look for the switch controlling the main circuit in your home. It shuts down every electrical device in your home, so be prepared for the darkness by carrying a flashlight. Make sure the power is completely off so you don’t have to worry about the risk of electrical shock. Also, unplug anything connected to the circuit controlled by the fuse you wish to replace.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*Test the power by attempting to turn on the electricity in different rooms. The lights and any plugged-in devices won’t turn on if the power is out.",
                    "#*The switch will usually be labeled in your fuse box. If the fuses aren’t labeled in your home, look for the biggest switch or keep flipping switches until you find the one you need. Consider labeling the fuse box components so you know where to look next time.",
                    "#Twist the fuse counterclockwise to remove it from the box. Home fuses screw in like light bulbs, which makes them super easy to remove and replace. The fuses are usually labeled by room, so find the one that controls the part of your home that lost power. Take it out of the box to replace it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ewD2FHev_pk&feature=youtu.be&t=97</ref>",
                    "#*More modern homes use [[Change a Circuit Breaker|circuit breakers]] instead of old-fashioned fuses. To reactivate a tripped breaker, all you have to do is flip it back to the “on” position. If it still doesn’t work, you can unscrew it, pull the wires out, and replace it.",
                    "#Test different fuses by if you don’t know which one broke. So your fuse box isn’t labeled and you’re stuck staring at a bunch of fuses that all look the same. Select and remove a fuse as you normally would, but then turn the power back on. Also, turn on the main switch and check your home to see which circuit didn’t reactivate. By doing this with each fuse, you can find the one responsible for the power outage.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bHQMCovwwAo&feature=youtu.be&t=147</ref>",
                    "#*For example, if you’re looking for the kitchen fuse, remove a fuse and test it. If it deactivates a different room, put it back in and try again. Remember to turn the main power off each time you touch the fuses.",
                    "#*A blown fuse may also look scorched or broken. If you notice a damaged fuse, you can zero in on the source of the problem.",
                    "#Swap the fuse out for one with the same power rating. Check the fuse to find a number on it indicating its amp rating. Getting a matching fuse is very important for safety reasons. If you can, take the broken fuse with you to the store to ensure you get an identical match. Then, plug the fuse in and turn it clockwise to lock it in place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ewD2FHev_pk&feature=youtu.be&t=159</ref>",
                    "#*Check online for replacement fuses or visit your nearest hardware store.",
                    "#*The wrong fuse increases the chances of electrical damage or even house fires. Never use a stronger fuse than the one you have. It will overload the circuit with extra power.",
                    "#*Plug fuses are commonly rated as 15, 20, and 30-amp. The 15-amp one is for lighting, the 20 is for outlets, and the 30 is for heavy appliances.",
                    "#Turn the power back on to test the new fuse. Once you have the fuse plugged in, double-check to make sure you disconnected all your electrical devices from the broken circuit. That means your toaster, your phone charger, and anything else you can think of. When you’re ready, flip the main fuse switch to restore power to your home. Test the circuit by going back and plugging all your electronics back in.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/wiring-a-breaker-box/</ref>",
                    "#*If the electricity doesn’t work at all, you most likely didn’t activate the electricity. Unplug everything again, then take another look at your fuse box. Make sure the fuse all fit tightly into their slots before flipping the main control switch to on.",
                    "#Plug your devices in individually if the fuse blows again. Sometimes the power will go out not long after you replace a blown fuse. That usually means that one of your devices is overloading the circuit. To find out which one it is, purchase another replacement fuse. After installing the new fuse, plug each item in one at a time until the fuse blows again.<ref>https://www.bbc.com/bitesize/guides/z886b82/revision/2</ref>",
                    "#*Unplug each device before plugging another one back in. Your devices could be faulty instead of the fuse itself. If none of them trip the fuse, then you might have overloaded the circuit with too many devices.",
                    "#*If you still can’t find the problem, call an experienced electrician. It could mean that your electrical wires are faulty.",
                    "",
                    "===Testing and Replacing a Home Fuse===",
                    "*Identical replacement fuse",
                    "*Flashlight",
                    "__METHODS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "When a potentially dangerous electrical surge occurs, a car fuse can “blow.” Breaking the electrical current protects the valuable components in your car from overheating. Even better, fuses are cheap and can be replaced without any special mechanical knowledge. You can test most fuses simply by looking at them, or you can perform more thorough tests with electronics such as a test light or multimeter. Once you have identified a broken fuse, you can replace it with an identical fuse to get your car running again.",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs===",
                    "===Using a Test Light===",
                    "===Testing with a Multimeter===",
                    "===Replacing a Fuse===",
                    "__PARTS__"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "26498682",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "16:42, 2 May 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,861 bytes)",
            "change": 178,
            "summary": "(added greenboxes)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Look under the car’s hood or dash board. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Many manufacturers choose to keep them near the car’s engine or battery. Other manufacturer’s like to hide the fuse boxes inside the car, beneath the steering wheel. Look for a gray or black box, usually with the word “Fuses” printed on it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' The fuse boxes can be in different locations. You may have 1 underneath the car’s hood and another inside the car.}}",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected in the fuse box. Don’t remove the fuse yet! This can be dangerous if the car is on. Removing a functioning fuse may also cause minor problems to the connected systems. Instead, examine each fuse while it is in place inside the box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5 Version 5.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Note:''' Removing a working fuse can erase data stored in the car’s diagnostic system. This is not an issue unless you need to get your car repaired, as the mechanic will use that data to assess the issue.}}",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle or an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 7 Version 5.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' If you have a multimeter, you can use it instead of a test light.}}",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On 2-prong fuses, you can find these holes on the top side at both ends.. Slide the end of the probe into the fuse’s port. Repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' If your car component doesn’t work but the fuse still does, the problem may be somewhere else. You can use the probe to test objects like brake switches.}}",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, testing how much current goes through. Make sure the mutimeter’s dial points to this setting.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the option listed as Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>}}",
                    "#Purchase an identical replacement fuse. Head to an auto supply store to find fuses for your car. Getting there may be tricky if your car is disabled due to a blown fuse. However, you should bring the fuse with you to ensure you get a proper match. The new fuse should be the same type, shape, and amp rating as your old fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 20.jpg|center]]{{greenbox: '''Tip:''' Car fuses are color-coded, so if the fuses are a different color, you know right away they aren’t a match.}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Look under the car’s hood or dash board. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Many manufacturers choose to keep them near the car’s engine or battery. Other manufacturer’s like to hide the fuse boxes inside the car, beneath the steering wheel. Look for a gray or black box, usually with the word “Fuses” printed on it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The fuse boxes can be in different locations. You may have 1 underneath the car’s hood and another inside the car.",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected in the fuse box. Don’t remove the fuse yet! This can be dangerous if the car is on. Removing a functioning fuse may also cause minor problems to the connected systems. Instead, examine each fuse while it is in place inside the box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Removing a working fuse can erase data stored in the car’s diagnostic system. This problem isn’t a big deal unless you need to get your car repaired.",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle or an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 7 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you have a multimeter, you can use it instead of a test light.",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On 2-prong fuses, you can find these holes on the top side at both ends.. Slide the end of the probe into the fuse’s port. Repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*If your car component doesn’t work but the fuse still does, the problem may be somewhere else. You can use the probe to test objects like brake switches.",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, testing how much current goes through. Make sure the mutimeter’s dial points to this setting.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the option listed as Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>",
                    "#Purchase an identical replacement fuse. Head to an auto supply store to find fuses for your car. Getting there may be tricky if your car is disabled due to a blown fuse. However, you should bring the fuse with you to ensure you get a proper match. The new fuse should be the same type, shape, and amp rating as your old fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 20.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Car fuses are color-coded, so if the fuses are a different color, you know right away they aren’t a match."
                ]
            }
        },
