        {
            "id": "25710743",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "04:59, 6 December 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,694 bytes)",
            "change": 2417,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Look under the car’s hood or dash board. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Many manufacturers choose to keep them near the car’s engine or battery. Other manufacturer’s like to hide the fuse boxes inside the car, beneath the steering wheel. Look for a gray or black box, usually with the word “Fuses” printed on it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check your car or repair manual if you can’t find the fuse boxes. These manuals come with new cars and are brimming with time-saving information, such as the location of the fuse boxes. This is helpful if your fuse boxes seem to be hidden pretty well. Some car manufacturers put fuse boxes in tricky locations such as in the glove box, near the brake pedal, in the trunk, or under the rear seat.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 2 Version 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the lid from the fuse box by pulling the latch. Look for a small latch jutting out from the lid’s side. Slide your fingers underneath the latch. Then, pull the latch towards you to easily pop the lid off of the fuse box.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 3-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Read the fuse box label on the back of the lid. Most fuse boxes have a diagram on the inside portion of the lid. It will list what system each fuse powers, it’s amp rating, and its corresponding location in the fuse box. This is very useful, since each fuse box will have around 20 fuses.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 4 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected in the fuse box. Don’t remove the fuse yet! This can be dangerous if the car is on. Removing a functioning fuse may also cause minor problems to the connected systems. Instead, examine each fuse while it is in place inside the box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check fuses for broken wires or scorch marks. Take a flashlight with you so the damaged components are easier to see. There are 2 types of fuses you may encounter, but they both operate the same way. If the inside of the fuse is black, you know it is broken. If it is clear, look inside it to see if the wire is still whole.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 6-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 6 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 6 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle or an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 7 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clamp the black grounding lead to a piece of metal. The clamp will be at the end of the test light opposite from the incandescent bulb. Squeeze the end of the clamp to open the jaws. Then, slide the jaws over a metal component near the fuse box. Any metal piece on the car will work, so you don’t need to stretch for any components out of your range.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 8-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn the key in the ignition to start the car’s electrical system. Turn the key so it is in the “Run” position. This should activate the car’s electrical systems, such as the radio and door lights, without starting the engine. You can test this by attempting to turn on the radio if it isn’t already on.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 9-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Test the light by touching its probe to a power source. You can operate the test light by pressing the tip down onto different electrical components. To test the light, you will need to find something you know is in working order and has power running through it. The easiest option, if you can reach it, is the car battery’s positive terminal. The test light should glow immediately.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On 2-prong fuses, you can find these holes on the top side at both ends.. Slide the end of the probe into the fuse’s port. Repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn the ignition key to shut the car off. Turning off the engine shuts off the car’s electrical supply. If you have an electric car, also make sure the car is not connected to a charging port. Keep the power off to avoid a nasty electric shock!<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 12-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove the fuse with a pair of needle-nose pliers. Needle-nose pliers are small and have long noses, making them perfect for dealing with those tiny fuses in your car. First, open the fuse box if you haven’t already by pulling the latch. Squeeze the handles to open up the pliers and clamp them onto the fuse. Pull the fuse upwards to lift it out of the fuse box without any struggling.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 13-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, testing how much current goes through. Make sure the mutimeter’s dial points to this setting.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the 2 probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout should change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the probes to the ends of the fuse. Hold onto the fuse as you press the probes against the metal terminals on the fuse’s bottom end. Trying to juggle the wires and the small fuse at the same time can be tricky at first, but keep at it. If the fuse is till good, the multimeter’s needle or number will change like it did when both probes were touching.<ref>https://www.freeasestudyguides.com/check-blown-fuse.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 16-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 16 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Shut the car’s power off by turning the ignition key. The car’s engine needs to be off, not running while you install the replacement. A running current can lead to a painful shock. Take precautions to avoid turning a simple repair into a costly hospital visit.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 17-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 17 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pull the cover off of the fuse box to check the diagram. Lift the latch on the side of the fuse box to loosen the cover. You will see a diagram somewhere on the box, usually on the lid. This diagram shows you where each fuse is located in the box as well as their amperage rating.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 18-preview Version 2.jpg|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the box using needle-nose pliers. Old fuses are easy to remove. The biggest challenge is their small size, but needle-nose pliers are designed to grasp small components. Fit the pliers on the fuse, then lift the fuse out of the box.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 19-preview Version 4.jpg|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 5.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 19 Version 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Purchase an identical replacement fuse. Head to an auto supply store to find fuses for your car. Getting there may be tricky if your car is disabled due to a blown fuse. However, you should bring the fuse with you to ensure you get a proper match. The new fuse should be the same type, shape, and amp rating as your old fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 20.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pop the new fuse into your fuse box. Setting the new fuse in the box is painless. Make sure the car is still off, then set the fuse in the empty slot. Press down with a little pressure until it pops into place. You’re already done and can turn on your car to test it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 21.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 21-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 21.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 21.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 21.360p.first.gif}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Look under the car’s hood or dash board. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Many manufacturers choose to keep them near the car’s engine or battery. Other manufacturer’s like to hide the fuse boxes inside the car, beneath the steering wheel. Look for a gray or black box, usually with the word “Fuses” printed on it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check your car or repair manual if you can’t find the fuse boxes. These manuals come with new cars and are brimming with time-saving information, such as the location of the fuse boxes. This is helpful if your fuse boxes seem to be hidden pretty well. Some car manufacturers put fuse boxes in tricky locations such as in the glove box, near the brake pedal, in the trunk, or under the rear seat.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove the lid from the fuse box by pulling the latch. Look for a small latch jutting out from the lid’s side. Slide your fingers underneath the latch. Then, pull the latch towards you to easily pop the lid off of the fuse box.",
                    "#Read the fuse box label on the back of the lid. Most fuse boxes have a diagram on the inside portion of the lid. It will list what system each fuse powers, it’s amp rating, and its corresponding location in the fuse box. This is very useful, since each fuse box will have around 20 fuses.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected in the fuse box. Don’t remove the fuse yet! This can be dangerous if the car is on. Removing a functioning fuse may also cause minor problems to the connected systems. Instead, examine each fuse while it is in place inside the box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check fuses for broken wires or scorch marks. Take a flashlight with you so the damaged components are easier to see. There are 2 types of fuses you may encounter, but they both operate the same way. If the inside of the fuse is black, you know it is broken. If it is clear, look inside it to see if the wire is still whole.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle or an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 8 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Clamp the black grounding lead to a piece of metal. The clamp will be at the end of the test light opposite from the incandescent bulb. Squeeze the end of the clamp to open the jaws. Then, slide the jaws over a metal component near the fuse box. Any metal piece on the car will work, so you don’t need to stretch for any components out of your range.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref>",
                    "#Turn the key in the ignition to start the car’s electrical system. Turn the key so it is in the “Run” position. This should activate the car’s electrical systems, such as the radio and door lights, without starting the engine. You can test this by attempting to turn on the radio if it isn’t already on.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>",
                    "#Test the light by touching its probe to a power source. You can operate the test light by pressing the tip down onto different electrical components. To test the light, you will need to find something you know is in working order and has power running through it. The easiest option, if you can reach it, is the car battery’s positive terminal. The test light should glow immediately.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 9 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On 2-prong fuses, you can find these holes on the top side at both ends.. Slide the end of the probe into the fuse’s port. Repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn the ignition key to shut the car off. Turning off the engine shuts off the car’s electrical supply. If you have an electric car, also make sure the car is not connected to a charging port. Keep the power off to avoid a nasty electric shock!{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove the fuse with a pair of needle-nose pliers. Needle-nose pliers are small and have long noses, making them perfect for dealing with those tiny fuses in your car. First, open the fuse box if you haven’t already by pulling the latch. Squeeze the handles to open up the pliers and clamp them onto the fuse. Pull the fuse upwards to lift it out of the fuse box without any struggling.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, testing how much current goes through. Make sure the mutimeter’s dial points to this setting.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 13 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Touch the 2 probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout should change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Touch the probes to the ends of the fuse. Hold onto the fuse as you press the probes against the metal terminals on the fuse’s bottom end. Trying to juggle the wires and the small fuse at the same time can be tricky at first, but keep at it. If the fuse is till good, the multimeter’s needle or number will change like it did when both probes were touching.<ref>https://www.freeasestudyguides.com/check-blown-fuse.html</ref>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Shut the car’s power off by turning the ignition key. The car’s engine needs to be off, not running while you install the replacement. A running current can lead to a painful shock. Take precautions to avoid turning a simple repair into a costly hospital visit.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pull the cover off of the fuse box to check the diagram. Lift the latch on the side of the fuse box to loosen the cover. You will see a diagram somewhere on the box, usually on the lid. This diagram shows you where each fuse is located in the box as well as their amperage rating.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the box using needle-nose pliers. Old fuses are easy to remove. The biggest challenge is their small size, but needle-nose pliers are designed to grasp small components. Fit the pliers on the fuse, then lift the fuse out of the box.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase an identical replacement fuse. Head to an auto supply store to find fuses for your car. Getting there may be tricky if your car is disabled due to a blown fuse. However, you should bring the fuse with you to ensure you get a proper match. The new fuse should be the same type, shape, and amp rating as your old fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pop the new fuse into your fuse box. Setting the new fuse in the box is painless. Make sure the car is still off, then set the fuse in the empty slot. Press down with a little pressure until it pops into place. You’re already done and can turn on your car to test it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
