            "reverted_by": "WikiHowUser20210524222356",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Always make sure the car is off before using the multimeter, since live circuits are dangerous.",
                    "#*If you plan on testing more than 1 fuse in a car's fuse box, take a photograph first so you know exactly where each fuse goes.",
                    "#*Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the option listed as Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>",
                    "#Touch the 2 probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout should change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*All the fuses are different sizes, so use the size along with the diagram to pick the correct fuse. Inspect it for damage before replacing it.",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, make sure the car’s power is turned off before handling a multimeter or any fuses."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*If you plan on testing more than one fuse in a car's fuse box, take a photograph first so you know exactly where each fuse goes.",
                    "#*Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the one that says Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>",
                    "#Touch the two probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout should change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*All the fuses are different sizes, so make sure you remove the right one. Inspect it for damage before replacing it.",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, make sure the car’s power is turned off before handling any fuses."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "24690108",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "15:59, 3 June 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,008 bytes)",
            "change": 1922,
            "summary": "(Editing/adding to change article to car fuses only as requested.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "WikiHowUser20210524222356",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "When a potentially dangerous electrical surge occurs, a car fuse can “blow.” Breaking the electrical current protects the valuable components in your car from overheating. Even better, fuses are cheap and can be replaced without any special mechanical knowledge. You can test most fuses simply by looking at them, or you can perform more thorough tests with electronics such as a test light or multimeter. Once you have identified a broken fuse, you can replace it with an identical fuse to get your car running again.",
                    "==Steps==",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs===",
                    "#Look under the car’s hood or dash board. Many cars have 2 fuse boxes, but there is no universal standard for their placement. Many manufacturers choose to keep them near the car’s engine or battery. Other manufacturer’s like to hide the fuse boxes inside the car, beneath the steering wheel. Look for a gray or black box, usually with the word “Fuses” printed on it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Pop your vehicle’s hood to find the box located near the battery or engine.",
                    "#*The fuse boxes can be in different locations. You may have 1 underneath the car’s hood and another inside the car.",
                    "#Check your car or repair manual if you can’t find the fuse boxes. These manuals come with new cars and are brimming with time-saving information, such as the location of the fuse boxes. This is helpful if your fuse boxes seem to be hidden pretty well. Some car manufacturers put fuse boxes in tricky locations such as in the glove box, near the brake pedal, in the trunk, or under the rear seat.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you don’t have your manual, search for your car’s make and model online.",
                    "#*If a part of your car isn’t working properly, use the manual to go directly to the fuse responsible for the part that lost power.",
                    "#Remove the lid from the fuse box by pulling the latch. Look for a small latch jutting out from the lid’s side. Slide your fingers underneath the latch. Then, pull the latch towards you to easily pop the lid off of the fuse box.",
                    "#*Some lids need to be rotated before they can be pulled off of the fuse box.",
                    "#Read the fuse box label on the back of the lid. Most fuse boxes have a diagram on the inside portion of the lid. It will list what system each fuse powers, it’s amp rating, and its corresponding location in the fuse box. This is very useful, since each fuse box will have around 20 fuses.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*For example, a square in the diagram’s upper left corner might have the word “radio” printed on it, so you know the fuse for the radio is in the upper left corner of the box.",
                    "#*If your lid doesn’t have a diagram, you can always find the fuse box diagram in the manual.",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected in the fuse box. Don’t remove the fuse yet! This can be dangerous if the car is on. Removing a functioning fuse may also cause minor problems to the connected systems. Instead, examine each fuse while it is in place inside the box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you find yourself having a hard time identifying damaged fuses, you can pull fuses out of the box. Make sure the car’s ignition is turned off first.",
                    "#*Removing a working fuse can erase data stored in the car’s diagnostic system. This problem isn’t a big deal unless you need to get your car repaired.",
                    "#Check fuses for broken wires or scorch marks. Take a flashlight with you so the damaged components are easier to see. There are 2 types of fuses you may encounter, but they both operate the same way. If the inside of the fuse is black, you know it is broken. If it is clear, look inside it to see if the wire is still whole.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The most common type of fuse is a 2-pronged plastic chip. It will have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs.",
                    "#*An older type of fuse is the transparent glass or plastic cylinder. It has a wire running end to end.",
                    "===Using a Test Light===",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle or an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 8 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Never test car wiring with an incandescent bulb that draws power from the circuit. This style of test light can trigger the airbags, causing massive car damage.",
                    "#*If you have a multimeter, you can use it instead of a test light.",
                    "#Clamp the black grounding lead to a piece of metal. The clamp will be at the end of the test light opposite from the incandescent bulb. Squeeze the end of the clamp to open the jaws. Then, slide the jaws over a metal component near the fuse box. Any metal piece on the car will work, so you don’t need to stretch for any components out of your range.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref>",
                    "#*A great place to clamp the grounding lead is on the battery’s negative terminal. It is the black terminal and may be marked with a “-” sign.",
                    "#*Note that newer LED models may not have a clamp and you do not need to attach them to anything.",
                    "#Turn the key in the ignition to start the car’s electrical system. Turn the key so it is in the “Run” position. This should activate the car’s electrical systems, such as the radio and door lights, without starting the engine. You can test this by attempting to turn on the radio if it isn’t already on.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>",
                    "#*Make sure the engine stays off. You don’t want a full electrical current coursing through the car’s parts while you’re working on them.",
                    "#Test the light by touching its probe to a power source. You can operate the test light by pressing the tip down onto different electrical components. To test the light, you will need to find something you know is in working order and has power running through it. The easiest option, if you can reach it, is the car battery’s positive terminal. The test light should glow immediately.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-use-a-test-light-circuit-tester</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 9 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The positive terminal will be red with a “+” sign marked on it.",
                    "#*You can also touch the probe to a working fuse or the positive battery cable’s metal connectors.",
                    "#Touch the probe to the fuse to see if the bulb lights up. Every fuse has a pair of testing ports. On 2-prong fuses, you can find these holes on the top side at both ends.. Slide the end of the probe into the fuse’s port. Repeat this with the other side. If the bulb lights up both times, the fuse still works.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*For cylindrical fuses, touch the probe against both ends.",
                    "#*If neither end causes the bulb to light up, make sure the black lead wire is grounded and that power is running to the fuse box.",
                    "#*If your car component doesn’t work but the fuse still does, the problem may be somewhere else. You can use the probe to test objects like brake switches.",
                    "===Testing with a Multimeter===",
                    "#Turn the ignition key to shut the car off. Turning off the engine shuts off the car’s electrical supply. If you have an electric car, also make sure the car is not connected to a charging port. Keep the power off to avoid a nasty electric shock!{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Remove the fuse with a pair of needle-nose pliers. Needle-nose pliers are small and have long noses, making them perfect for dealing with those tiny fuses in your car. First, open the fuse box if you haven’t already by pulling the latch. Squeeze the handles to open up the pliers and clamp them onto the fuse. Pull the fuse upwards to lift it out of the fuse box without any struggling.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>",
                    "#*You may be able to lift the fuses out with your fingernails, but prying out these miniscule fuses by hand can be a challenge.",
                    "#*A regular pair of pliers or tweezers may work, or you can even use the end of a paperclip to leverage the fuse.",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega, or '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, testing how much current goes through. Make sure the mutimeter’s dial points to this setting.<ref>http://www.learningaboutelectronics.com/Articles/How-to-test-a-fuse</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 13 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Analog multimeters have multiple Ω settings. Choose the one that says Ωx1 or Rx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>",
                    "#Touch the two probes together to test the multimeter. The multimeter will have a black and red wire hanging from it. Each wire has a metal probe at the end. When you touch the probes together, the multimeter’s readout should change to show 0 resistance.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/electrical/how-to-use-a-multimeter/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Your multimeter will have a dial if it is an analog model or a button if it is digital. You can use this to calibrate the meter back to 0 if it gives you a false reading.",
                    "#Touch the probes to the ends of the fuse. Hold onto the fuse as you press the probes against the metal terminals on the fuse’s bottom end. Trying to juggle the wires and the small fuse at the same time can be tricky at first, but keep at it. If the fuse is till good, the multimeter’s needle or number will change like it did when both probes were touching.<ref>https://www.freeasestudyguides.com/check-blown-fuse.html</ref>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*You can pop a good fuse right back into the fuse box.",
                    "#*If the number doesn’t change, your fuse is busted. You will need to replace it.",
                    "#Shut the car’s power off by turning the ignition key. The car’s engine needs to be off, not running while you install the replacement. A running current can lead to a painful shock. Take precautions to avoid turning a simple repair into a costly hospital visit.<ref>https://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Take the key out of the ignition to make sure the car is off. You can also try turning on the radio to see if the power is off.",
                    "#*If you have an electric car, detach any charging cables from outlets.",
                    "#Pull the cover off of the fuse box to check the diagram. Lift the latch on the side of the fuse box to loosen the cover. You will see a diagram somewhere on the box, usually on the lid. This diagram shows you where each fuse is located in the box as well as their amperage rating.<ref>https://www.esquire.com/lifestyle/cars/how-to/a34947/how-to-change-a-car-fuse/</ref>",
                    "#*You will need to know the amperage rating in order to get a new fuse.",
                    "#*If you can’t locate a diagram, look in the owner’s manual or repair manual for more information.",
                    "#Remove the fuse from the box using needle-nose pliers. Old fuses are easy to remove. The biggest challenge is their small size, but needle-nose pliers are designed to grasp small components. Fit the pliers on the fuse, then lift the fuse out of the box.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*All the fuses are different sizes, so make sure you remove the right one. Inspect it for damage before replacing it.",
                    "#Purchase an identical replacement fuse. Head to an auto supply store to find fuses for your car. Getting there may be tricky if your car is disabled due to a blown fuse. However, you should bring the fuse with you to ensure you get a proper match. The new fuse should be the same type, shape, and amp rating as your old fuse.<ref>https://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/view-all/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Car fuses are color-coded, so if the fuses are a different color, you know right away they aren’t a match.",
                    "#*The amp rating is the number written on the fuse. If the fuse has the wrong amp rating, it can short-circuit or cause damage to your car.",
                    "#*Transparent cylindrical fuses come in 2 types, a straight fast-blow wire and a coiled slow-blow wire. Remember to match the wire shapes in your new and old fuse.",
                    "#Pop the new fuse into your fuse box. Setting the new fuse in the box is painless. Make sure the car is still off, then set the fuse in the empty slot. Press down with a little pressure until it pops into place. You’re already done and can turn on your car to test it.<ref>https://www.dmv.org/how-to-guides/fuse.php</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Glass fuses sometimes need to be installed by pushing 1 end into place at a time.",
                    "*Fuses age like any other car part. Over time, they will fail. It doesn’t mean your car has a major problem.",
                    "*If you need to drive the car, such as to pick up a new fuse, replace the broken fuse with a fuse from a nonessential system, such as the radio. Make sure both fuses have identical ampere ratings.",
                    "*Keep a few fuses stored in your car so you can make a quick repair when necessary.",
                    "*If the replacement fuse blows shortly after installation, your car’s electrical system may have a more serious issue. Consult an mechanic.",
                    "*To avoid electrical shock, make sure the car’s power is turned off before handling any fuses.",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs of Damage===",
                    "*Flashlight",
                    "",
                    "===Using a Test Light===",
                    "*Test light",
                    "",
                    "===Testing with a Multimeter===",
                    "*Needle-nose pliers",
                    "===Replacing a Fuse===",
                    "*New fuse",
                    "*Needle-nose pliers"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "A fuse is designed to protect more important parts of an electrical system from overheating and related damage. When a potentially dangerous current surge occurs, the wire running through the fuse \"blows\" and cuts the connection to the circuit. This is great for protecting your car or home electrical system, but inconvenient in the short term. Armed with the right tool and a little knowledge, you'll be able to check your fuses quickly when this happens and find out whether you need a replacement.",
                    "",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "===Locating the Fuse Box===",
                    "#Check your car manual or look in likely places. Many cars have two fuse boxes, and there's no universal standard for where they are placed. You'll save time if you can refer to your car's manual (or [http://www.eautorepair.net/app/YearsMakes.asp find the manual online]), look up the location of the fuse that controls the part of the car that lost power, and check it directly. If you don't have the manual, check these common locations for a large box, or a group of exposed fuses:<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Most cars have one or two fuse boxes under the hood, next to the engine or battery. There may also be a fuse box inside the car, so keep looking if the fuse you're looking for isn't here, or if all the fuses here are fine.",
                    "#*Newer cars often have a fuse box underneath the dash in an easily accessible location. Check the ceiling of the glove box for a hinge that swings downward. You may need a flat-head screwdriver to open the lid.",
                    "#*Older cars often have their fuses in an open box to the left of the brake pedal or the foot-operated parking brake. It's difficult to examine the fuses closely in some models, so bring a flashlight and/or hand mirror.",
                    "#*Less commonly, the fuse box is located in the trunk or under the rear seat.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref>",
                    "#Look for indoor fuse boxes. If you are checking the fuses in your home, look for a fuse box or circuit box in closets, basements, laundry rooms, or on the outside wall of the house. If you live in an apartment or shared home, the fuse box may be located in a neighbor's apartment.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check the user's manual for other devices. If you are checking the fuses in a central air conditioning unit or another appliance, check the user manual to find out where they are. For some appliances, you may need to shut off the power to the device before you can safely access the fuse box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "===Checking Visual Signs===",
                    "#Read the labels if present. Car fuse boxes often have a diagram on the outside or inside lid, and always in the car's user manual. This can save you a lot of time, since you can check the one fuse that protects the radio (or other nonfunctional system) instead of all 40+ fuses. Home fuse boxes are often unlabeled, but typically only have a few fuses, so it's easy to test them all[[Image:Check Fuses Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Find your car's user manual or fuse box diagram online [http://www.eautorepair.net/app/YearsMakes.asp here] or with a general online search. You'll need to know the make and model of your car.",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected. Don't remove the fuse yet, as this can be dangerous if the power is still connected, and can cause minor problems to connected systems if you remove a still-functioning fuse. Instead, look closely at each fuse while they are in place.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check for broken wires or scorch marks. A fuse can look completely normal and still be blown (and in need of replacement), but more often than not there will be a visual sign that will guide you to the problem fuse. Fuses come in three basic forms:",
                    "#*Transparent cylinders (glass or plastic) have a wire running through the middle. If the wire is broken, the fuse is blown. If the whole cylinder is scorched black or brown, the fuse is blown and there may be a major short on the circuit that needs repair (especially if the replacement fuse blows after a short time).[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The two-pronged chip fuses often used in cars have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs, inside the plastic. If the wire is broken, the fuse is blown, but it's often difficult to tell.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Opaque cylinders (covered by a solid metal coating) must be tested by other means.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off power and remove an indoor fuse. If checking an indoor fuse, you can turn off the main power to the house and remove the fuse to get a closer look. For any type of fuse, if you are still unsure, you can move on to the next section. If you have identified the problem fuse, skip ahead to replacing the fuse.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*This step is not recommended at this stage for car fuses, since removing the wrong fuse can negatively affect idling performance, diagnostic information, or accessories.<ref>http://www.agcoauto.com/content/news/p2_articleid/245</ref>",
                    "===Testing the Circuit===",
                    "====With a Test Light====",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle, or with an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet. ''Never'' test car wiring with an old-style test light with an incandescent bulb that draws power from the circuit, or you could trigger the airbags and cause massive damage.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 8 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Alternatively, if you have a multimeter, skip down to the instructions below to use it instead.",
                    "#Check the fuse with the test light. Follow these steps to check the fuse with a test light:<ref>http://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 9 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Clamp the black grounding lead to an electrically conductive object (such as anything metal).",
                    "#*Turn the ignition to run, or make sure the main power supply is on if testing a house fuse.",
                    "#*Touch the red probe near one end of the fuse, then the other. (For small two-pronged fuses, the two prongs are the two ends of the fuse.)",
                    "#Interpret the results. If the fuse is good, the bulb in the test light will light up both times. If it does not light up on one end, the fuse is blown and needs to be replaced.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*If ''neither'' end lights up, there is no current running to the fuse box, or your black lead is not grounded, or the light bulb has burned out. Correct this problem and try again, or use a multimeter instead.",
                    "====With a Multimeter====",
                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Turn off the ignition to the car, or the power leading to the home fuse box. Pry the fuse out on one end, then the other. You may need tweezers or needle-nose pliers to remove the fuse, or a similar tool sometimes attached to the lid of automobile fuse boxes.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Test continuity if available. Most digital multimeters have a continuity setting, which is labeled with a series of parallel arcs: <font size=1>)</font><font size=2>)</font><font size=3>)</font>. Turn the dial to this setting, then place the two probes at opposite ends of the fuse. If you hear a constant beep while they are touching the fuse, the fuse is still good. If you don't hear anything, the fuse is blown.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 12 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If your multimeter has no continuity option, or you would like to double-check the result, continue on to test resistance.",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega: '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, and tests how much current goes through. We don't really care about the exact resistance reading, but if the fuse is blown, we won't get any resistance reading because the current will not be able to pass through the broken wire.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 13 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you have an analog multimeter, there are multiple Ω settings. Choose the one that says Ωx1.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref> Old models sometimes say Rx1 instead.",
                    "#Touch the two probes to each other. Touch the two metal probes together and look at the multimeter display. This number (or needle position) is what the multimeter currently \"thinks\" is a resistance of 0. If we get a result near this number when testing the fuse, the fuse is still good.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 14 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Your multimeter probably has a dial (analog multimeters) or a button (digital) that lets you calibrate this back to 0. You can do this if you plan on using the multimeter a lot, but it's not necessary for this task.",
                    "#Touch the probes to each end of the fuse. Touch one probe to each end of the fuse, while looking at the display. If the display doesn't change when you touch the fuse, the fuse is blown and needs to be replaced. If the number or needle position moves to a similar position to the one you saw when both probes were touching, then the fuse is still good and can be popped back into the fuse box.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview Version 3.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 4.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15 Version 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Whenever you are removing a fuse or putting in a new one, make sure the power supply to the fuse box is off. For a car, this means turning off the ignition.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a new fuse. These are available at hardware stores, electronic shops, or auto supply stores (for car fuses). Bring along your old, blown fuse so you can compare the size and other characteristics, as described below.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Select a new fuse of the same amp rating, type, and shape. Replacing a fuse with the exact same type of fuse is very important. Most importantly, check the amp rating, normally a number written on the fuse, and purchase a replacement fuse with the same rating. Each fuse is designed to blow at a specific number of amps; this is its entire purpose. If you use too low an amp rating, the fuse might blow during normal use and short the circuit. If you use too high an amp rating, the fuse won't blow during a power surge, and the less replaceable parts of the circuit will be damaged instead.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Transparent, cylindrical fuses come in two types: fast-blow (a straight wire), or slow-blow (a coiled wire). Don't put in a slow-blow unless the old fuse was also a slow-blow, or the fuse may not be able to prevent damage quickly enough.<ref>http://www.electronicrepairguide.com/how-to-test-a-fuse.html</ref>",
                    "#Pop in the new fuse. Modern fuses should pop in easily with a little pressure. Old, glass fuses sometimes need to have one end pushed in first, then the other.<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 19.jpg|center]]",
                    "*Fuses age just like everything else. Over time, they will fail. A blown fuse is not always an indicator that there is something wrong with the power circuit.",
                    "*If a blown fuse is preventing your car from working, but you need to drive to pick up a new fuse, consult your car's manual and remove a fuse with the same ampere rating from a nonessential system, such as the radio. Put it in place of the broken fuse.<ref>https://techinfo.honda.com/rjanisis/pubs/om/JA0505/JA0505O00353A.pdf</ref>",
                    "*If the replacement fuse blows shortly after installation, and you're sure you used the correct amp rating, there could be a more serious issue with your electrical system. Consult an electrician.<ref>http://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse/page/2</ref>",
                    "*Make sure that the power is turned off to the equipment before testing any fuse or removing any fuse, unless you are using a test light.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
