                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Whenever you are removing a fuse or putting in a new one, make sure the power supply to the fuse box is off. For a car, this means turning off the ignition.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 16.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 16-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 16.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 16.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Find a new fuse. These are available at hardware stores, electronic shops, or auto supply stores (for car fuses). Bring along your old, blown fuse so you can compare the size and other characteristics, as described below.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 17.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 17-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 17.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 17.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Select a new fuse of the same amp rating, type, and shape. Replacing a fuse with the exact same type of fuse is very important. Most importantly, check the amp rating, normally a number written on the fuse, and purchase a replacement fuse with the same rating. Each fuse is designed to blow at a specific number of amps; this is its entire purpose. If you use too low an amp rating, the fuse might blow during normal use and short the circuit. If you use too high an amp rating, the fuse won't blow during a power surge, and the less replaceable parts of the circuit will be damaged instead.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 18.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 18-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 18.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 18.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pop in the new fuse. Modern fuses should pop in easily with a little pressure. Old, glass fuses sometimes need to have one end pushed in first, then the other.<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 19.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 19-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 19.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 19.360p.first.gif}}"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "19917858",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "19:43, 30 March 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,161 bytes)",
            "change": 2666,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Wikiphoto",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Check your car manual or look in likely places. Many cars have two fuse boxes, and there's no universal standard for where they are placed. You'll save time if you can refer to your car's manual (or [http://www.eautorepair.net/app/YearsMakes.asp find the manual online]), look up the location of the fuse that controls the part of the car that lost power, and check it directly. If you don't have the manual, check these common locations for a large box, or a group of exposed fuses:<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 1.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 1-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Look for indoor fuse boxes. If you are checking the fuses in your home, look for a fuse box or circuit box in closets, basements, laundry rooms, or on the outside wall of the house. If you live in an apartment or shared home, the fuse box may be located in a neighbor's apartment.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 2.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 2-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 2 Version 3.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Check the user's manual for other devices. If you are checking the fuses in a central air conditioning unit or another appliance, check the user manual to find out where they are. For some appliances, you may need to shut off the power to the device before you can safely access the fuse box.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 3.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 3-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 3 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Read the labels if present. Car fuse boxes often have a diagram on the outside or inside lid, and always in the car's user manual. This can save you a lot of time, since you can check the one fuse that protects the radio (or other nonfunctional system) instead of all 40+ fuses. Home fuse boxes are often unlabeled, but typically only have a few fuses, so it's easy to test them all<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 4.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 4-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 4 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected. Don't remove the fuse yet, as this can be dangerous if the power is still connected, and can cause minor problems to connected systems if you remove a still-functioning fuse. Instead, look closely at each fuse while they are in place.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 5.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 5-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 5 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Check for broken wires or scorch marks. A fuse can look completely normal and still be blown (and in need of replacement), but more often than not there will be a visual sign that will guide you to the problem fuse. Fuses come in three basic forms:<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 6.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 6-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 6.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 6.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 6.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Transparent cylinders (glass or plastic) have a wire running through the middle. If the wire is broken, the fuse is blown. If the whole cylinder is scorched black or brown, the fuse is blown and there may be a major short on the circuit that needs repair (especially if the replacement fuse blows after a short time).",
                    "#*The two-pronged chip fuses often used in cars have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs, inside the plastic. If the wire is broken, the fuse is blown, but it's often difficult to tell.",
                    "#*Opaque cylinders (covered by a solid metal coating) must be tested by other means.",
                    "#Turn off power and remove an indoor fuse. If checking an indoor fuse, you can turn off the main power to the house and remove the fuse to get a closer look. For any type of fuse, if you are still unsure, you can move on to the next section. If you have identified the problem fuse, skip ahead to replacing the fuse.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 7.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 7-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 7 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 7.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 7.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle, or with an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet. ''Never'' test car wiring with an old-style test light with an incandescent bulb that draws power from the circuit, or you could trigger the airbags and cause massive damage.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 8.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 8-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 8 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 8.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 8.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Check the fuse with the test light. Follow these steps to check the fuse with a test light:<ref>http://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 9.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 9-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 9 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 9.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 9.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Interpret the results. If the fuse is good, the bulb in the test light will light up both times. If it does not light up on one end, the fuse is blown and needs to be replaced.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 10.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 10-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Turn off the ignition to the car, or the power leading to the home fuse box. Pry the fuse out on one end, then the other. You may need tweezers or needle-nose pliers to remove the fuse, or a similar tool sometimes attached to the lid of automobile fuse boxes.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 11.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 11-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 11 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 11.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 11.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Test continuity if available. Most digital multimeters have a continuity setting, which is labeled with a series of parallel arcs: <font size=1>)</font><font size=2>)</font><font size=3>)</font>. Turn the dial to this setting, then place the two probes at opposite ends of the fuse. If you hear a constant beep while they are touching the fuse, the fuse is still good. If you don't hear anything, the fuse is blown.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 12.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 12-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 12 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega: '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, and tests how much current goes through. We don't really care about the exact resistance reading, but if the fuse is blown, we won't get any resistance reading because the current will not be able to pass through the broken wire.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 13.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 13-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 13 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 13.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 13.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the two probes to each other. Touch the two metal probes together and look at the multimeter display. This number (or needle position) is what the multimeter currently \"thinks\" is a resistance of 0. If we get a result near this number when testing the fuse, the fuse is still good.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 14.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 14-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 14 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 14.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 14.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Touch the probes to each end of the fuse. Touch one probe to each end of the fuse, while looking at the display. If the display doesn't change when you touch the fuse, the fuse is blown and needs to be replaced. If the number or needle position moves to a similar position to the one you saw when both probes were touching, then the fuse is still good and can be popped back into the fuse box.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 15.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 15-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 15 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 15.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 15.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Whenever you are removing a fuse or putting in a new one, make sure the power supply to the fuse box is off. For a car, this means turning off the ignition.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 16.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 16-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 16 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 16.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 16.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Find a new fuse. These are available at hardware stores, electronic shops, or auto supply stores (for car fuses). Bring along your old, blown fuse so you can compare the size and other characteristics, as described below.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 17.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 17-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 17 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 17.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 17.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Select a new fuse of the same amp rating, type, and shape. Replacing a fuse with the exact same type of fuse is very important. Most importantly, check the amp rating, normally a number written on the fuse, and purchase a replacement fuse with the same rating. Each fuse is designed to blow at a specific number of amps; this is its entire purpose. If you use too low an amp rating, the fuse might blow during normal use and short the circuit. If you use too high an amp rating, the fuse won't blow during a power surge, and the less replaceable parts of the circuit will be damaged instead.<br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 18.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 18-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 18 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 18.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 18.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pop in the new fuse. Modern fuses should pop in easily with a little pressure. Old, glass fuses sometimes need to have one end pushed in first, then the other.<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Check Fuses Step 19.360p.mp4|Check Fuses Step 19-preview.jpg|Check Fuses Step 19 Version 2.jpg|gif=Check Fuses Step 19.360p.gif|giffirst=Check Fuses Step 19.360p.first.gif}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Check your car manual or look in likely places. Many cars have two fuse boxes, and there's no universal standard for where they are placed. You'll save time if you can refer to your car's manual (or [http://www.eautorepair.net/app/YearsMakes.asp find the manual online]), look up the location of the fuse that controls the part of the car that lost power, and check it directly. If you don't have the manual, check these common locations for a large box, or a group of exposed fuses:<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Look for indoor fuse boxes. If you are checking the fuses in your home, look for a fuse box or circuit box in closets, basements, laundry rooms, or on the outside wall of the house. If you live in an apartment or shared home, the fuse box may be located in a neighbor's apartment.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check the user's manual for other devices. If you are checking the fuses in a central air conditioning unit or another appliance, check the user manual to find out where they are. For some appliances, you may need to shut off the power to the device before you can safely access the fuse box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Read the labels if present. Car fuse boxes often have a diagram on the outside or inside lid, and always in the car's user manual. This can save you a lot of time, since you can check the one fuse that protects the radio (or other nonfunctional system) instead of all 40+ fuses. Home fuse boxes are often unlabeled, but typically only have a few fuses, so it's easy to test them all[[Image:Check Fuses Step 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Leave the fuse connected. Don't remove the fuse yet, as this can be dangerous if the power is still connected, and can cause minor problems to connected systems if you remove a still-functioning fuse. Instead, look closely at each fuse while they are in place.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check for broken wires or scorch marks. A fuse can look completely normal and still be blown (and in need of replacement), but more often than not there will be a visual sign that will guide you to the problem fuse. Fuses come in three basic forms:",
                    "#*Transparent cylinders (glass or plastic) have a wire running through the middle. If the wire is broken, the fuse is blown. If the whole cylinder is scorched black or brown, the fuse is blown and there may be a major short on the circuit that needs repair (especially if the replacement fuse blows after a short time).[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The two-pronged chip fuses often used in cars have a U-shaped wire running between the prongs, inside the plastic. If the wire is broken, the fuse is blown, but it's often difficult to tell.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Opaque cylinders (covered by a solid metal coating) must be tested by other means.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 6Bullet3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off power and remove an indoor fuse. If checking an indoor fuse, you can turn off the main power to the house and remove the fuse to get a closer look. For any type of fuse, if you are still unsure, you can move on to the next section. If you have identified the problem fuse, skip ahead to replacing the fuse.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase a modern test light. You can purchase this tool at a hardware store or electronics store. Select a \"computer safe\" model with an LED light in the handle, or with an incandescent bulb powered by a battery or power outlet. ''Never'' test car wiring with an old-style test light with an incandescent bulb that draws power from the circuit, or you could trigger the airbags and cause massive damage.<ref>http://www.bcae1.com/testlite.htm</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Check the fuse with the test light. Follow these steps to check the fuse with a test light:<ref>http://www.2carpros.com/articles/how-to-check-a-car-fuse</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Interpret the results. If the fuse is good, the bulb in the test light will light up both times. If it does not light up on one end, the fuse is blown and needs to be replaced.<ref>http://www.familyhandyman.com/automotive/car-maintenance/replacing-auto-fuses/</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Turn off the ignition to the car, or the power leading to the home fuse box. Pry the fuse out on one end, then the other. You may need tweezers or needle-nose pliers to remove the fuse, or a similar tool sometimes attached to the lid of automobile fuse boxes.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Test continuity if available. Most digital multimeters have a continuity setting, which is labeled with a series of parallel arcs: <font size=1>)</font><font size=2>)</font><font size=3>)</font>. Turn the dial to this setting, then place the two probes at opposite ends of the fuse. If you hear a constant beep while they are touching the fuse, the fuse is still good. If you don't hear anything, the fuse is blown.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set the multimeter to test resistance. This setting looks like the Greek letter omega: '''Ω'''. The resistance setting sends a small current through the fuse, and tests how much current goes through. We don't really care about the exact resistance reading, but if the fuse is blown, we won't get any resistance reading because the current will not be able to pass through the broken wire.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 13.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Touch the two probes to each other. Touch the two metal probes together and look at the multimeter display. This number (or needle position) is what the multimeter currently \"thinks\" is a resistance of 0. If we get a result near this number when testing the fuse, the fuse is still good.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 14.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Touch the probes to each end of the fuse. Touch one probe to each end of the fuse, while looking at the display. If the display doesn't change when you touch the fuse, the fuse is blown and needs to be replaced. If the number or needle position moves to a similar position to the one you saw when both probes were touching, then the fuse is still good and can be popped back into the fuse box.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off the power and remove the fuse. Whenever you are removing a fuse or putting in a new one, make sure the power supply to the fuse box is off. For a car, this means turning off the ignition.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a new fuse. These are available at hardware stores, electronic shops, or auto supply stores (for car fuses). Bring along your old, blown fuse so you can compare the size and other characteristics, as described below.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Select a new fuse of the same amp rating, type, and shape. Replacing a fuse with the exact same type of fuse is very important. Most importantly, check the amp rating, normally a number written on the fuse, and purchase a replacement fuse with the same rating. Each fuse is designed to blow at a specific number of amps; this is its entire purpose. If you use too low an amp rating, the fuse might blow during normal use and short the circuit. If you use too high an amp rating, the fuse won't blow during a power surge, and the less replaceable parts of the circuit will be damaged instead.[[Image:Check Fuses Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pop in the new fuse. Modern fuses should pop in easily with a little pressure. Old, glass fuses sometimes need to have one end pushed in first, then the other.<ref>http://www.edmunds.com/how-to/how-to-inspect-and-replace-automotive-fuses.html</ref>[[Image:Check Fuses Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
